export default "# Post Craft Rules\n\nStructural rules for editing any LinkedIn post, drawn from one creator's corpus. **Whatever register the writer is in (earnest, technical, wry, blunt) make it land harder. Never swap it.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. CRAFT PRINCIPLES\n\n**1. Enter flat and cold.** Open on a statement, quote, or concrete detail. Never a question.\n*Evidence:* Flat declaratives dominate the top tier (4,967; 2,580; 1,853); a rhetorical setup question flopped at 46. Named dead openers: \"I've been thinking about…\"\n*Before:* \"Ever wonder why discharge takes so long?\" → *After:* \"The patient is ready in twenty minutes. The paperwork takes four hours.\"\n\n**2. Lead with the human, not the label.** Bury the org or event name; open on the person or claim.\n*Evidence:* A program's name as the opener: 51 and 61. The same program, led by one person's decision to quit her job: 238, roughly 4x, same promotional intent.\n*Before:* \"Our Hospitality Summit is Tuesday. Register now.\" → *After:* \"A front desk agent talked a guest out of a one-star review. She speaks Tuesday.\"\n\n**3. Name the specific.** Replace abstractions with an exact number, time, or object.\n*Evidence:* \"Tuesday at 4:58 pm\" (1,427), \"checked it 43 times\" (2,580). A post whose payoff was the word \"disappointing\" scored 46, too vague.\n*Before:* \"A complicated shift change caused problems.\" → *After:* \"Three nurses handed off eleven patients using one dying whiteboard marker.\"\n\n**4. Complete the thought.** Short is fine; fragmentary is not. Every post needs its second half.\n*Evidence:* A two-line setup/payoff hit 1,515; same-length fragments died (48, 42). Length follows structure: escalations need 500–700 characters (2,837), reversals under 150 words (2,580).\n*Before:* \"Contractors, you know what I mean.\" → *After:* \"Contractors: the client picks the tile after the tile is installed.\"\n\n**5. Stand alone from the attachment.** The text must hold up with the image or link removed.\n*Evidence:* Two words over a surprising image: 898 eng / 147K impressions. Visual-dependent captions collapsed (50, 41, 40).\n*Before:* \"😂 so true\" [meme] → *After:* \"Someone laminated the break room microwave rules. All nine of them.\" [photo]\n\n**6. Escalate in plausible steps.** Each beat outdoes the last while staying believable.\n*Evidence:* A recursive escalation post hit 2,837: every turn worse, all of it realistic, and readers extended the chain in comments.\n*Before:* \"Retail in December is chaos.\" → *After:* \"Dec 1: we build the display. Dec 8: a customer rebuilds it. Dec 15: he's training other customers.\"\n\n**7. Land smaller than the setup.** End on understatement, not a summary or moral.\n*Evidence:* The top post (4,967) ends a career-change narrative with \"Seems to be working out on both fronts.\" Another closes on \"Probably user error.\" (865). Over-explaining is a named failure mode.\n*Before:* \"…and that's why leadership is about listening. Thoughts?\" → *After:* \"She'd said it in every meeting for a year. We just wrote it down this time.\"\n\n**8. Make the last line add something.** A closer must reframe or extend; if it restates, cut it.\n*Evidence:* Reframing closers hit 1,746 and 1,479. Where the body already landed, the two that close without one (4,967; 1,445) did fine. A closer that only plugs something: 98.\n*Before:* \"…and that's how we cut wait times. Efficiency matters!\" → *After:* \"…and that's how we cut wait times. We also lost the fish tank.\"\n\n**9. Hold one register.** Don't graft a punchline onto a sincere post, or sincerity onto a light one.\n*Evidence:* The two straight posts (4,967; 1,445) end without a punchline; forcing one into an earnest post is a named failure. Mixing breeds hedging: \"I think maybe,\" \"sort of.\" Cut those.\n*Before:* \"My grandmother passed last month. Anyway, here's what that taught me about procurement.\" → *After:* \"My grandmother passed last month. She taught me to weigh flour, not scoop it. I still do.\"\n\n**10. Build participation in, don't bolt it on.** Engagement comes from structure readers want to extend or argue with.\n*Evidence:* The escalation post drew comments extending it (2,837); one line over a divisive image drew 235. The bolted-on ask scored 51.\n*Before:* \"Best budgeting tip for small restaurants? Comment below!\" → *After:* \"Every restaurant has one dish quietly subsidizing the menu. Ours is the side salad.\"\n\n**11. Assume zero prior context.** Any in-group reference needs an on-ramp for a stranger.\n*Evidence:* Context-dependent posts died: a second-language post (42), a video-dependent post (41), a recap assuming familiarity (51).\n*Before:* \"Another SOAP note battle with the EHR today.\" → *After:* \"Charting a fifteen-minute visit takes twenty-two minutes. The software was sold as a time-saver.\"\n\n**12. Delete the institutional voice.** Cut anything that sounds drafted by a comms team.\n*Evidence:* The avoid-list ends on posts that sound \"written by a marketing team.\" Logistics-only posts are the worst category (51, 61).\n*Before:* \"Thrilled to announce a strategic initiative to elevate the customer journey.\" → *After:* \"We stopped making people call to cancel. It took two years to approve.\"\n\n---\n\n## 2. DIAGNOSTIC QUESTIONS\n\nRun in order on the post text alone; each \"no\" flags a principle.\n\n1. Is the first line a statement, quote, or concrete detail, not a question or announcement? → P1\n2. Does the first sentence offer a stranger something interesting, not an org or event name? → P2\n3. Does the post contain at least one exact number, time, name, or object? → P3\n4. Does it have both a setup and a payoff? Would it work alone on screen? → P4\n5. If an image, video, or link is referenced, is the text still interesting without it? → P5\n6. With three or more beats, does each raise stakes while staying believable? → P6\n7. Is the final line smaller and more concrete than the build-up, not a summary? → P7\n8. Does the final line say something the body didn't? → P8\n9. Does the post hold one register, with no hedges (\"I think maybe,\" \"sort of\")? → P9\n10. Is engagement invited by structure rather than an appended question? → P10\n11. Could a first-time reader follow every reference? → P11\n12. Is it free of announcement-speak and logistics with no reason to care? → P12\n\n---\n\n## 3. EDIT TYPES\n\n- **Rewrite the opening line:** *Q1 fails.* Pull the most concrete sentence up front; make questions declarative. Give the line verbatim.\n- **Bury the lede:** *Q2 fails.* Move the org or event name later in the post.\n- **Replace an abstraction with a specific:** *Q3 fails.* Name the vague word, ask for the fact (\"'a long wait': how long?\"). Supply a slot, never a number.\n- **Add the missing half:** *Q4 fails.* Say whether setup or payoff is missing; supply its shape.\n- **Make the text attachment-independent:** *Q5 fails.* Give the caption its own claim; keep it short.\n- **Reorder for escalation:** *Q6 fails.* Sequence beats weakest to strongest; dial back any that breaks plausibility.\n- **Cut the final line or paragraph:** *Q7/Q8 fail.* End it a line or two earlier; say what the cut was doing.\n- **Rewrite the closer to add a beat:** *Q8 fails, closer wanted.* Propose a line that reframes, deflates, or extends.\n- **Cut the hedge / restore the register:** *Q9 fails.* Delete qualifiers; remove the graft, don't rewrite the passage.\n- **Remove the engagement ask:** *Q10 fails.* Replace with a contestable claim or open pattern.\n- **Add the on-ramp:** *Q11 fails.* One clause of context for the jargon; never delete the reference.\n- **Strip institutional voice:** *Q12 fails.* Rewrite plainly, no longer; put a reason-to-care sentence ahead of logistics.\n- **Cut repetition:** *bloat.* Remove sentences restating a neighbor; never cut toward a word target.\n\n---\n\n## 4. ANTI-GUIDANCE\n\nNever suggest:\n\n- **A tone the writer isn't already using.** The top two posts (4,967; 4,627) and the layoff post (1,445) are played entirely straight. Register is the writer's; craft is ours.\n- **Hashtags, decorative emoji, \"thoughts?\", \"comment below.\"** Named failure devices; the appended-ask post scored 51.\n- **An inspirational or lesson-shaped ending.** The best endings here are deliberately anticlimactic.\n- **A rhetorical question opener.** The most consistently documented underperformer.\n- **Hype vocabulary:** \"thrilled,\" \"game-changing,\" \"unlock,\" \"leverage,\" \"journey.\"\n- **Invented specifics.** Ask for the real number; these details land because they're true.\n- **Polishing away imperfect phrasing.** \"Authentic over polished\" is explicit; the top post uses casual bullets and a mid-sentence ellipsis.\n- **Removing a distinctive recurring element** (a signature closer or running theme) as \"unprofessional.\" A callback post hit 1,479. Flag it only if it restates the body or only plugs something (98).\n- **Lengthening a short post that's already complete.** 1,515 engagements on two lines.\n- **Credential lists, frameworks, or numbered \"key takeaways.\"** Lists need a narrative engine.\n- **Optimizing for impressions.** One post drew 98K impressions on 232 engagements; reach is not resonance.\n- **Softening a committed claim.** Full commitment landed at 1,479; the post that hedged its premise scored 46.\n";
